<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Broërec</h1><p class="st-meta">550 CE – 850 CE (approx.) · capital Vannes</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Breton kingdom of the Vannes region, named the land of Waroch after a warlord who defied the Franks.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the 6th century Breton rulers extended their power over the Vannetais, the former land of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ancient-veneti">Veneti</a>, which remained largely Gallo-Roman. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a> records the counts <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macliau">Macliau</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waroch-ii">Waroch</a> fighting for control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vannes">Vannes</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waroch-ii">Waroch</a> repeatedly raided Frankish territory around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rennes">Rennes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Nantes</a> and defeated or bought off Frankish armies sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guntram">Guntram</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chilperic-i">Chilperic I</a> in the 570s and 580s. The region kept a strong Gallo-Roman element alongside the Breton settlers, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vannes">Vannes</a> remained a bishopric.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Broërec remained the border zone between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bretons">Bretons</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/franks">Franks</a> until it was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-brittany">Kingdom of Brittany</a> created by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nominoe">Nominoe</a>, who was count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vannes">Vannes</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a>. Its name survives in the Breton name of the Vannes region.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/broerec.jpg" alt="Broërec" width="320" height="245"><figcaption>Image: France_Pays_bretons_map.jpg: Mikael Bodlore-Penlaez derivative work: Pethrus (talk) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:France_Pays_bretons_map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>550 CE – 850 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vannes</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Macliau, Waroch II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Breton, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bro Waroch, Vannetais</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=700&amp;focus=broerec" aria-label="Broërec on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4616 508 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Broërec lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4936 764L4930 753"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4930 753h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4936 764h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4936 764h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Vannes. <a href="/atlas?year=700&amp;focus=broerec">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
